Consider each coordinate separately.

For $x$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x &=4 - t^{2} \\ &=4 - t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The square takes every nonnegative value. Equality occurs at $t=0$. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $x\le 4$.

For $y$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y &=- t^{2} + 4 t - 8 \\ &=- \left(t - 2\right)^{2} - 4
\end{aligned}
$$

The square takes every nonnegative value. Equality occurs at $t=2$. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $y\le -4$.

Therefore, $x\le 4,\,y\le -4$.

Consider each coordinate separately.

For $x$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x &=-\sqrt{- t}+4
\end{aligned}
$$

The radicand starts at $1$ and increases without bound along the allowed half-line. The square root is increasing, and its exterior coefficient is $-1$. The boundary $t=-1$ gives $x=3$ and is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $x\le 3$.

For $y$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y &=-2\sqrt{3 - t}
\end{aligned}
$$

The radicand starts at $4$ and increases without bound along the allowed half-line. The square root is increasing, and its exterior coefficient is $-2$. The boundary $t=-1$ gives $y=-4$ and is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $y\le -4$.

Therefore, $x\le 3,\,y\le -4$.
